<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter9_raw-pointers-and-references">
    <title>Raw Pointers and References</title>

    <p>
        Chapter 3 introduced references as a way for a function to work with
        the caller's object. This section compares references with
        <term>raw pointers</term>, the built-in pointer types such as
        <c>int*</c>. Both can provide indirect access to an object, but their
        rules and uses are different.
    </p>

    <p>
        A raw pointer is a separate object that stores a pointer value. That
        value can identify another object, identify no object through
        <c>nullptr</c>, or be changed to identify a different object. A
        reference is an alias bound to an existing object.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter9_raw-pointers-and-references_syntax-reassignment">
        <title>Syntax and Reassignment</title>

        <p>
            A pointer must be explicitly dereferenced to change the value of
            the object it identifies. Assigning a new address to the pointer
            changes which object it identifies.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_chapter9_raw_pointer_reassignment"
                 language="cpp">
            <code><![CDATA[
int x = 10;
int y = 20;

int* pointer = &x;  // pointer stores the address of x
*pointer = 15;      // x is now 15
pointer = &y;       // pointer now identifies y
*pointer = 25;      // y is now 25
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            The statement <c>pointer = &amp;y</c> changes the pointer's stored
            address. The statement <c>*pointer = 25</c> changes the object at
            that address.
        </p>

        <p>
            A reference acts as another name for the object to which it is
            bound. It cannot be reseated to refer to a different object.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_chapter9_reference_reassignment"
                 language="cpp">
            <code><![CDATA[
int x = 10;
int y = 20;

int& reference = x;  // reference is another name for x
reference = y;       // assigns y's value to x
                     // reference still refers to x
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            In the final assignment, <c>reference = y</c> does not make
            <c>reference</c> refer to <c>y</c>. It copies the value of
            <c>y</c> into the object that <c>reference</c> already identifies,
            so <c>x</c> becomes <c>20</c>.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table_chapter9_pointer_reference_syntax">
            <title>Pointer and reference syntax</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="38%"/>
                <col width="62%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>Expression</cell>
                    <cell>Meaning</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>int* pointer = &amp;x;</c></cell>
                    <cell>Declare a pointer and store the address of <c>x</c>.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>*pointer = 15;</c></cell>
                    <cell>Access the object identified by <c>pointer</c> and assign to it.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>pointer = &amp;y;</c></cell>
                    <cell>Change the pointer so it identifies <c>y</c>.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>int&amp; reference = x;</c></cell>
                    <cell>Declare <c>reference</c> as an alias for <c>x</c>.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>reference = y;</c></cell>
                    <cell>Assign the value of <c>y</c> to <c>x</c>; it does not reseat the reference.</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter9_raw-pointers-and-references_ampersand">
        <title>Two Meanings of <c>&amp;</c></title>

        <p>
            The symbol <c>&amp;</c> has different meanings depending on its
            context. In an expression, it is the address-of operator. In a
            declaration, it can indicate a reference type.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_chapter9_pointer_reference_ampersand"
                 language="cpp">
            <code><![CDATA[
int score = 90;

int* pointer = &score;  // & obtains score's address
int& reference = score; // & declares reference as an alias
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            The address-of expression <c>&amp;score</c> produces a pointer value.
            The declaration <c>int&amp; reference</c> says that
            <c>reference</c> is a reference to an existing <c>int</c> object.
            These uses look similar, but they are not the same operation.
        </p>

        <p>
            The symbol <c>*</c> also has two related uses: in a declaration it
            helps declare a pointer type, and in an expression it dereferences
            a pointer.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_chapter9_pointer_reference_star"
                 language="cpp">
            <code><![CDATA[
int* pointer;  // pointer declaration
*pointer;      // dereference expression
            ]]></code>
        </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter9_raw-pointers-and-references_comparison">
        <title>Comparison of Raw Pointers and References</title>

        <table xml:id="table_chapter9_raw_pointer_reference_comparison">
            <title>Raw pointers and references</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="27%"/>
                <col width="36.5%"/>
                <col width="36.5%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>Feature</cell>
                    <cell>Raw pointer</cell>
                    <cell>Reference</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Initialization</cell>
                    <cell>May be uninitialized, but an uninitialized pointer is dangerous. It can also be initialized to <c>nullptr</c>.</cell>
                    <cell>Must be bound to an object when it is declared.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Nullability</cell>
                    <cell>Can represent no object with <c>nullptr</c>.</cell>
                    <cell>Has no ordinary null value.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Reseating</cell>
                    <cell>Can be changed to identify another object.</cell>
                    <cell>Cannot be changed to refer to another object.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Access syntax</cell>
                    <cell>Uses <c>*</c> or <c>-&gt;</c> to access the identified object.</cell>
                    <cell>Uses ordinary variable or member-access syntax.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Pointer arithmetic</cell>
                    <cell>Possible within one array object, including a one-past-the-end position.</cell>
                    <cell>Not applicable.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Lifetime safety</cell>
                    <cell>Can become null, uninitialized, or dangling.</cell>
                    <cell>Can become dangling if its referred object is destroyed.</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            A reference is not a second copy of the object. It is another way
            to name the same object. A raw pointer is a separate object that
            stores a pointer value. The C++ language does not require a
            particular machine-level representation for references, so it is
            better to focus on their language rules rather than assume that a
            reference is always exactly a hidden pointer.
        </p>

        <interactive xml:id="interactive_chapter9_raw_pointer_reference"
                     iframe="https://pythontutor.com/iframe-embed.html#code=%23include%20%3Ciostream%3E%0A%0Aint%20main%28%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20int%20x%20%3D%2010%3B%0A%20%20%20%20int%20y%20%3D%2020%3B%0A%0A%20%20%20%20int*%20pointer%20%3D%20%26x%3B%0A%20%20%20%20*pointer%20%3D%2015%3B%0A%20%20%20%20pointer%20%3D%20%26y%3B%0A%20%20%20%20*pointer%20%3D%2025%3B%0A%0A%20%20%20%20int%26%20reference%20%3D%20x%3B%0A%20%20%20%20reference%20%3D%20y%3B%0A%0A%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20%22x%20%3D%20%22%20%3C%3C%20x%20%3C%3C%20%27%5Cn%27%3B%0A%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20%22y%20%3D%20%22%20%3C%3C%20y%20%3C%3C%20%27%5Cn%27%3B%0A%0A%20%20%20%20return%200%3B%0A%7D&amp;codeDivHeight=400&amp;codeDivWidth=420&amp;cppShowMemAddrs=true&amp;curInstr=1&amp;origin=opt-frontend.js&amp;py=cpp_g%2B%2B9.3.0"
                     width="100%"
                     aspect="16:10">
            <description>
                <p>
                    C++ Tutor visualization comparing pointer reassignment with
                    assignment through a reference.
                </p>
            </description>
        </interactive>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter9_raw-pointers-and-references_function-parameters">
        <title>Function Parameters</title>

        <p>
            Both pointers and references can allow a function to work with an
            object owned by its caller. The choice communicates an important
            part of the function's interface.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_chapter9_pointer_reference_parameters"
                 language="cpp">
            <code><![CDATA[
void increase_value(int& value) {
    ++value;
}

void increase_value(int* pointer) {
    if (pointer != nullptr) {
        ++(*pointer);
    }
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            These functions cannot be overloaded together because their
            parameter types can create confusing calls in some situations.
            More importantly, they communicate different contracts:
            <c>increase_value(int&amp;)</c> requires an existing object, while
            <c>increase_optional_value(int*)</c> can receive <c>nullptr</c> and
            therefore must decide what that means. Although pointer and
            reference parameter overloads are allowed, using both for the same
            function name can make an interface harder to read and should be
            done only when the distinction is clear.
        </p>

        <p>
            When a function only needs to inspect a large object, a
            <c>const</c> reference is often a good choice:
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_chapter9_const_reference_parameter"
                 language="cpp">
            <code><![CDATA[
void print_score(const int& score) {
    std::cout << score << '\n';
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            For small types such as <c>int</c>, passing by value is usually
            simpler. The reference-versus-pointer choice becomes especially
            useful when objects are large, optional, mutable through a function,
            or connected to dynamic storage.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter9_raw-pointers-and-references_guidance">
        <title>When to Use Which</title>

        <p>
            A practical rule is to use a reference when the function requires
            a valid object and does not need to change which object is being
            referred to. Use a pointer when the function must represent
            <q>no object</q>, change its target, perform pointer-based traversal,
            or interact with an interface that uses pointers.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table_chapter9_raw_pointer_reference_guidance">
            <title>Choosing between a raw pointer and a reference</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="37%"/>
                <col width="63%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>Situation</cell>
                    <cell>Recommended starting point</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>The function requires a valid object.</cell>
                    <cell>Use a reference parameter, such as <c>const T&amp;</c> for read-only access.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>The argument is optional.</cell>
                    <cell>Use a pointer and define what <c>nullptr</c> means.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>The function must modify the caller's object.</cell>
                    <cell>Use <c>T&amp;</c> when the object must exist; use <c>T*</c> when no object is also allowed.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>The target may change during traversal.</cell>
                    <cell>Use a pointer, because pointers can be reseated.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>The function owns dynamic storage.</cell>
                    <cell>Do not use a raw pointer merely to express ownership. Later, prefer an owning smart pointer such as <c>std::unique_ptr</c> when appropriate.</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            A raw pointer does not automatically own the object to which it
            points. It may point to an ordinary local object, an array element,
            or dynamically allocated storage. Ownership and lifetime must be
            designed separately. This is one reason that code using
            <c>new</c> and <c>delete</c> requires careful rules about which part
            of a program is responsible for releasing storage.
        </p>

        <p>
            Also remember that pointer arithmetic is not general arithmetic on
            arbitrary addresses. It is defined for positions within the same
            array object, including the one-past-the-end position, which must
            not be dereferenced.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter9_raw-pointers-and-references_summary">
        <title>Summary</title>

        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>A raw pointer is a separate object that stores a pointer value.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>A reference is an alias bound to an existing object.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>A pointer can be null and reseated; a reference cannot normally be null or reseated.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p><c>pointer = &amp;object</c> changes a pointer's target, while <c>reference = object</c> assigns through an existing reference.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>Use references for required objects and pointers when optionality, reseating, or pointer-based traversal matters.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>Neither a pointer nor a reference automatically communicates ownership.</p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </subsection>

    <exercise label="chapter9_raw_pointer_reference_1">
        <statement>
            <p>What does <c>pointer = &amp;y;</c> do?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>It changes <c>pointer</c> so that it identifies <c>y</c>.</p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. The pointer is reseated to store the address of <c>y</c>.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>It copies the value of <c>y</c> into the object previously identified by <c>pointer</c>.</p></statement><feedback><p>That would require dereferencing the pointer on the left side.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>It changes the type of <c>pointer</c>.</p></statement><feedback><p>A pointer's type does not change through assignment.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>It deletes <c>y</c>.</p></statement><feedback><p>Taking an address does not destroy an object.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter9_raw_pointer_reference_2">
        <statement>
            <p>What is the result of the following code?</p>
            <program line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
int x = 10;
int y = 20;
int& reference = x;
reference = y;
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><c>x</c> becomes 20, and <c>reference</c> still refers to <c>x</c>.</p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. Assignment through a reference changes the object to which it is already bound.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><c>reference</c> becomes an alias for <c>y</c>.</p></statement><feedback><p>References cannot be reseated by assignment.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><c>y</c> becomes 10.</p></statement><feedback><p>The assignment copies from <c>y</c> into the object identified by <c>reference</c>.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>The program creates a second copy of <c>x</c>.</p></statement><feedback><p>A reference is an alias, not a copied object.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter9_raw_pointer_reference_3">
        <statement>
            <p>Which parameter is most appropriate when a function requires a valid, read-only object and should avoid copying a large object?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice><statement><p><c>LargeObject object</c></p></statement><feedback><p>This passes a copy of the object.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><c>const LargeObject&amp; object</c></p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. A const reference avoids copying and expresses that the object is required but not modified.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><c>LargeObject* object</c></p></statement><feedback><p>A pointer allows <c>nullptr</c>, so the function would need a pointer contract and a safety check.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><c>LargeObject&amp;&amp; object</c></p></statement><feedback><p>This introduces rvalue references, which are not needed for this requirement.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter9_raw_pointer_reference_4"
              adaptive="yes"
              indentation="show">
        <statement>
            <p>Arrange the blocks to change an object through a pointer and then reseat the pointer.</p>
        </statement>
        <blocks>
            <block order="1"><cline>int first_value = 10;</cline></block>
            <block order="2"><cline>int second_value = 20;</cline></block>
            <block order="3"><cline>int* pointer = &amp;first_value;</cline></block>
            <block order="4"><cline>*pointer = 15;</cline></block>
            <block order="5"><cline>pointer = &amp;second_value;</cline></block>
            <block order="6"><cline>*pointer = 25;</cline></block>
        </blocks>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter9_raw_pointer_reference_5">
        <statement>
            <p>Which situation most strongly favors a pointer over a reference?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice><statement><p>The function requires a valid object and must not modify it.</p></statement><feedback><p>A const reference commonly expresses this contract.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>The function must represent an optional object that may be absent.</p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. A pointer can represent absence with <c>nullptr</c>.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>The function should always receive a valid object.</p></statement><feedback><p>A reference communicates that requirement more directly.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>The function should use ordinary variable syntax for access.</p></statement><feedback><p>References provide ordinary alias syntax; pointers require explicit access.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>
</section>
